{"page":710,"blocks":[{"x":0.04849,"y":0.05257,"w":0.41464,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01535,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"696 Chapter 11: Parametric Equations and Polar Coordinates"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.09309,"w":0.58891,"h":0.02899,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Solution We divide the numerator and denominator by 10 to put the equation in stan- dard polar form:"},{"x":0.59718,"y":0.12691,"w":0.07204,"h":0.04074,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"5>2 1 + cos u."},{"x":0.56635,"y":0.14698,"w":0.02339,"h":0.01346,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r ="},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.17452,"w":0.1286,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"This is the equation"},{"x":0.56356,"y":0.198,"w":0.10837,"h":0.02721,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":0,"t":"r = ke 1 + e cos u"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.22547,"w":0.4411,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"with k = 5>2 and e = 1. The equation of the directrix is x = 5>2."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.26658,"w":0.58883,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"From the ellipse diagram in Figure 11.52, we see that k is related to the eccentricity e and the semimajor axis a by the equation"},{"x":0.22863,"y":0.27084,"w":0.04725,"h":0.0225,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Directrix x = k"},{"x":0.14001,"y":0.29855,"w":0.04379,"h":0.02251,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"Focus at origin"},{"x":0.57592,"y":0.30926,"w":0.03983,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"k = a"},{"x":0.09823,"y":0.31082,"w":0.03489,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Center"},{"x":0.60805,"y":0.31579,"w":0.05162,"h":0.01763,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"e - ea."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.34191,"w":0.59302,"h":0.02956,"fs":0.01401,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"From this, we find that ke = a(1 - e2). Replacing ke in Equation (5) by a(1 - e2) gives the standard polar equation for an ellipse."},{"x":0.14971,"y":0.3618,"w":0.01235,"h":0.01023,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ea"},{"x":0.17522,"y":0.3918,"w":0.00654,"h":0.01923,"fs":0.01023,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"a e"},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.40766,"w":0.50139,"h":0.01293,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Polar Equation for the Ellipse with Eccentricity e and Semimajor Axis a"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.42156,"w":0.2304,"h":0.04298,"fs":0.01151,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 11.52 In an ellipse with semimajor axis a, the focus–directrix distance is k = (a>e) - ea, so"},{"x":0.56437,"y":0.43548,"w":0.10297,"h":0.02348,"fs":0.01401,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"r = a(1 - e2)"},{"x":0.5952,"y":0.44619,"w":0.28678,"h":0.01999,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"1 + e cos u (6)"},{"x":0.04931,"y":0.4671,"w":0.09648,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"ke = a(1 - e2)."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.50223,"w":0.52804,"h":0.01347,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Notice that when e = 0, Equation (6) becomes r = a, which represents a circle."},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.53964,"w":0.04105,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Lines"},{"x":0.32332,"y":0.56361,"w":0.59303,"h":0.04417,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"Suppose the perpendicular from the origin to line L meets L at the point P0(r0, u0), with r0 Ú 0 (Figure 11.53). Then, if P(r, u) is any other point on L, the points P, P0, and O are the vertices of a right triangle, from which we can read the relation"},{"x":0.55204,"y":0.61733,"w":0.13149,"h":0.01535,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"r0 = r cos (u - u0)."},{"x":0.35359,"y":0.67152,"w":0.52839,"h":0.04922,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"The Standard Polar Equation for Lines If the point P0(r0, u0) is the foot of the perpendicular from the origin to the line L, and r0 Ú 0, then an equation for L is"},{"x":0.1223,"y":0.69608,"w":0.03318,"h":0.01078,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"P(r, u)"},{"x":0.55286,"y":0.72841,"w":0.32912,"h":0.01499,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"r cos (u - u0) = r0. (7)"},{"x":0.16261,"y":0.74112,"w":0.05155,"h":0.01269,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"P0(r0, u0)"},{"x":0.12544,"y":0.75005,"w":0.01,"h":0.01215,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r0"},{"x":0.35277,"y":0.77779,"w":0.33406,"h":0.0251,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"For example, if u0 = p>3 and r0 = 2, we find that"},{"x":0.10558,"y":0.78569,"w":0.01119,"h":0.01269,"fs":0.01023,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"u0"},{"x":0.509,"y":0.81616,"w":0.09059,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"r cos au - p"},{"x":0.58976,"y":0.81616,"w":0.05293,"h":0.03035,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"3 b = 2"},{"x":0.04849,"y":0.827,"w":0.24944,"h":0.02762,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"FIGURE 11.53 We can obtain a polar equation for line L by reading the relation"},{"x":0.04931,"y":0.85785,"w":0.25352,"h":0.02884,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"r0 = r cos (u - u0) from the right triangle OP0P."},{"x":0.41329,"y":0.85964,"w":0.09419,"h":0.02323,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"r acos ucos p"},{"x":0.49765,"y":0.85964,"w":0.14508,"h":0.03035,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"3 + sin usin p 3 b = 2"},{"x":0.46164,"y":0.90724,"w":0.36842,"h":0.0276,"fs":0.01279,"n":5,"k":0,"t":"1 2r cos u + 23 2 r sin u = 2, or x + 23y = 4."}]}